# Caveman Skill Usage

Default: OFF.

Caveman shrinks output tokens only and adds about 1 to 1.5k input tokens
per turn. So the win is on prose-heavy replies, not on token count alone,
and on already terse or agent-heavy work it can go net negative.

## Enable Caveman when:
- User explicitly asks: "caveman", "brief", "short", "less talk"
- Quick coding questions
- Small bug fixes
- Simple refactoring
- Boilerplate generation
- Short code reviews
- Private notes or commit messages

## Disable Caveman when:
- Architecture or system design
- Complex debugging or investigations
- Production risk analysis
- Public documentation or communication
- Tutorials or learning explanations
- Multi-step instructions where details matter
- Long unattended agent passes over the repo
  (savings near zero, input overhead per turn dominates)

## Auto-off (even mid-session):
- Security-sensitive or irreversible operations, keep output fully explicit

When unsure, keep Caveman OFF.